From ff7cf61b59364af06b351cad99d3c17e8c7e5a8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:17:06 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 133/134] render: hoist a unit's declarations without regexes,
 once per text
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WERules::hoistDeclarations was most of a unit's preprocessing: per line it ran std::regex (the directive regex on
every # line, the declaration regex on every line starting with a storage keyword, the identifier regex over the
#if chains and the line), and libstdc++'s backtracking executor costs microseconds a line. A scene builds the same
unit texts many times (one effect shader serves many passes): one --lwe-smoke load of 3042492564 called it 508 times
for 59 distinct texts and spent 79 ms in it, 3516174947 136 ms, 3414858021 112 ms.

It now reads each line with a hand-written scan that accepts exactly what the regexes matched: the directive's
`[a-z]` run with no word character after it and the rest after its spaces; a declaration as whole words between
`\s` runs (a storage word, the qualifiers, a type word, a name), one optional `[…]` up to the first `]`, `;`, then
nothing or a `//` comment; identifiers as sregex_iterator lists them. The lines are views into the text (no copy per
line), and the result is remembered per text for the process (keyed by the whole text, so a hit is exactly what the
work would give; the health record of a hoist is reported again on a hit; bounded at 64 MB, about 0.8 MB for the
largest scene).

Same output, checked three ways: every hoistDeclarations input of the 93 library scenes the corpus plays (366
distinct texts, 193 of them hoisting something, from both builds with a dump hook) gives byte-identical output and
the same health records from the old and the new code, in the worker and offline; 2,000,000 generated texts built
from lines on the regexes' edges (whitespace kinds, qualifiers, near-miss types and names, brackets, comments,
directives, scope, include markers, #defines a declaration reads) and 300,000 mutants of the 366 real texts, each
through both: 0 differences (three deliberately broken variants of the scan differ in 315-835 of 20,000 generated
texts each).

Measured: hoistDeclarations per call 124 -> 14 us (the scan), 0.85 us on a repeat; one load of every corpus scene
2130 -> 114 ms in all. Apply to first frame on a private headless liascape-player (scene_load.py, medians of 16 warm
/ 4 cold / 4 after-a-boot loads per core, the cores alternating): 3042492564 warm 473 -> 418 ms, cold 1098 -> 1062,
boot 517 -> 440; 3163060610 402 -> 357, 731 -> 666, 450 -> 398; 2388299037 437 -> 417, 594 -> 592, 479 -> 443;
3414858021 637 -> 519, 805 -> 773, 644 -> 554; 3516174947 1005 -> 860, 1476 -> 1378, 1044 -> 945.

